Summary of Logical Relationships:

• **G1 → {G1.1, G1.2, G1.3} [OR] with runtime “#”:**  
  – Alternative A: G1.1 (Collection Phase) and G1.2 (Delivery Phase) executed sequentially (AND “;”).  
  – Alternative B: G1.3 (Parallel Collection and Delivery) executed in parallel (“#”).  
  **Justification:** The mission can be satisfied by either a sequential single-robot mission or by a parallel multi-robot mission if multiple resources are involved.

• **G1.1 → {G1.1.1, G1.1.2} [OR] with runtime “#”:**  
  – G1.1.1: Normal Collection (tasks AT1, AT2, AT3 in sequential order).  
  – G1.1.2: Low Battery Fallback for Collection (task AT4).  
  **Justification:** The collection phase must either follow the normal path or switch to a fallback when battery is low.

• **G1.1.1 → {AT1, AT2, AT3} [AND] with runtime “;”:**  
  **Justification:** Each step in normal collection must be executed sequentially.

• **G1.1.2 → {AT4} [AND] with runtime “;”:**  
  **Justification:** The fallback in collection is activated when battery is insufficient.

• **G1.2 → {G1.2.1, G1.2.2} [OR] with runtime “#”:**  
  – G1.2.1: Normal Delivery (tasks AT5 and AT6 in sequential order).  
  – G1.2.2: Low Battery Fallback for Delivery (tasks AT7 and AT8 in sequential order).  
  **Justification:** Delivery phase must either proceed normally or engage the fallback if battery drops to 30%.

• **G1.2.1 → {AT5, AT6} [AND] with runtime “;”:**  
  **Justification:** Navigation and delivery are performed sequentially.

• **G1.2.2 → {AT7, AT8} [AND] with runtime “;”:**  
  **Justification:** The fallback for delivery requires returning to checkpoint and reassigning the task.

• **G1.3 → {AT9, AT10, AT11} [AND] with runtime “#”:**  
  **Justification:** In the parallel mission branch, the tasks are executed concurrently to reduce overall mission time.

─────────────────────────────  
This design uses:
• The “#” operator to denote OR decompositions (where fulfilling one branch is sufficient).  
• The “;” operator to denote sequential (AND) execution.  
• For fallback alternatives within each phase, the OR operator is used so that if a condition (e.g., low battery) is met, the corresponding branch is selected.

